            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "I don't use it and am not sure if I can use it on Linux. How exactly does it not work?\r\n\r\nBut the basic idea of [this approach](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/discussions/1537#discussioncomment-11999468) looks about right and probably requires a bit more investigation about data structures. Are you sure that `cslsex.handler(...)` actually *returns* an array of items? I would think that it acts as described in `:h lsp-handler`, i.e. acts on LSP response. So it should probably be something along these lines:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n...\r\n        [\"textDocument/definition\"] = function(err, result, ctx)\r\n            if err ~= nil then error(string.format('Error code %s. %s', err.code, err.message) end\r\n            -- Process `result` into an array of items suitable for 'mini.pick'. See `:h MiniPick-source.items-common`\r\n            return MiniPick.start({ source = { items = items, names = 'Definition' } })\r\n        end,\r\n...\r\n```",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">I don't use it and am not sure if I can use it on Linux. How exactly does it not work?</p>\n<p dir=\"auto\">But the basic idea of <a href=\"https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/discussions/1537#discussioncomment-11999468\" data-hovercard-type=\"discussion\" data-hovercard-url=\"/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/discussions/1537/hovercard?comment_id=11999468\">this approach</a> looks about right and probably requires a bit more investigation about data structures. Are you sure that <code class=\"notranslate\">cslsex.handler(...)</code> actually <em>returns</em> an array of items? I would think that it acts as described in <code class=\"notranslate\">:h lsp-handler</code>, i.e. acts on LSP response. So it should probably be something along these lines:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight highlight-source-lua notranslate position-relative overflow-auto\" dir=\"auto\" data-snippet-clipboard-copy-content=\"...\n        [&quot;textDocument/definition&quot;] = function(err, result, ctx)\n            if err ~= nil then error(string.format('Error code %s. %s', err.code, err.message) end\n            -- Process `result` into an array of items suitable for 'mini.pick'. See `:h MiniPick-source.items-common`\n            return MiniPick.start({ source = { items = items, names = 'Definition' } })\n        end,\n...\"><pre class=\"notranslate\"><span class=\"pl-c1\">...</span>\n        [<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">\"</span>textDocument/definition<span class=\"pl-pds\">\"</span></span>] <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">function</span>(<span class=\"pl-smi\">err</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">result</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">ctx</span>)\n            <span class=\"pl-k\">if</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">err</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">~=</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">nil</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">then</span> <span class=\"pl-c1\">error</span>(<span class=\"pl-c1\">string.format</span>(<span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>Error code %s. %s<span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span></span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">err</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">code</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">err</span>.<span class=\"pl-e\">message</span>) <span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>\n            <span class=\"pl-c\"><span class=\"pl-c\">--</span> Process `result` into an array of items suitable for 'mini.pick'. See `:h MiniPick-source.items-common`</span>\n            <span class=\"pl-k\">return</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">MiniPick</span>.<span class=\"pl-c1\">start</span>({ <span class=\"pl-smi\">source</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> { <span class=\"pl-smi\">items</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-smi\">items</span>, <span class=\"pl-smi\">names</span> <span class=\"pl-k\">=</span> <span class=\"pl-s\"><span class=\"pl-pds\">'</span>Definition<span class=\"pl-pds\">' </span></span>} })\n        <span class=\"pl-k\">end</span>,\n<span class=\"pl-c1\">...</span></pre></div>",
            "bodyText": "I don't use it and am not sure if I can use it on Linux. How exactly does it not work?\nBut the basic idea of this approach looks about right and probably requires a bit more investigation about data structures. Are you sure that cslsex.handler(...) actually returns an array of items? I would think that it acts as described in :h lsp-handler, i.e. acts on LSP response. So it should probably be something along these lines:\n...\n        [\"textDocument/definition\"] = function(err, result, ctx)\n            if err ~= nil then error(string.format('Error code %s. %s', err.code, err.message) end\n            -- Process `result` into an array of items suitable for 'mini.pick'. See `:h MiniPick-source.items-common`\n            return MiniPick.start({ source = { items = items, names = 'Definition' } })\n        end,\n...",
